why this exist
==============

this is a machine people get by opening a pr, not by waiting on someone to
ssh in and click around. the metric is admin toil: how many times a human
has to touch the box per member. target is one review.

git is the identity provider. you open a pr that adds user-vms/you.nix,
someone reviews it, merge hits main, nix builds the rest: microvm guest,
ssh keys, quota, mesh join, public url. one object. git log answers who has
access, who granted it, when, and why. if that sounds slow, good. the
friction is the curriculum.

each member gets a real VM, not a shared unix account. your kernel, your
mess. the host only sees a guest.

no cloud
--------

a 2016 server off the used market does what a few thousand dollars a month
of cloud would. that is not the main point. the point is what each one
teaches. the cloud teaches you a vendor's api and its billing page. a box
in the room teaches watts, disks, packets and boot order. when compute is
fixed, the fastest way forward is better code, not a bigger invoice.

single master. no HA. no k8s. no terraform. one box, one flake, one
firewall, one switch. if it is down, it is down, and you find out why.

breakable because rebuildable
-----------------------------

nothing on the metal is precious. the raid is degraded and we left it that
way. a member can wreck their VM; an operator can wreck the host. that's
fine as long as git can put it back.

so the score is not uptime. the score is: wipe to working, from git, in how
many minutes. measure it, publish it, make it smaller. every breakage is a
drill. see docs/drills.md.

docs are back
-------------

we deleted the docs once so people would read the nix instead of pasting
a wiki into a chatbot. that was wrong. it locked out the people who were
trying to learn.

so docs/ exists, with one rule: docs explain why and how, the nix decides
what. every doc points at the file that is the truth. if the doc and the
repo disagree, the repo wins and the doc is a bug. fix it in the same pr.

how to learn
------------

1. docs/README.md, in order.
2. then hosts/mothership/ and modules/, top to bottom.
3. nix develop · nix flake check · nix eval options until the graph is in
   your head.
4. break your own VM first. then break the metal on purpose, and time the
   rebuild.
5. when something fails, read the journal and the nixos option before you
   ask anyone, model or human. ask about a line you can point at.
